FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/formatter.rs:445 — The formatter writes `lru_cache(n)`, then a newline, then `to`; the parser only recognises the decorator when `to` is the immediate next token (src/parser.rs:1230, src/parser.rs:1244), and the lexer emits a `Newline` token (src/lexer.rs:488). So `rb format` on a working cached function produces `lru_cache(50)` as an expression statement calling an unknown function followed by an ordinary uncached `to` declaration; running the formatted file fails with `Unknown function 'lru_cache'`. — Write the decorator and `to` on the same line (`lru_cache({capacity}) `), or make `lru_cache_declaration_ahead`/`parse_cached_function` skip newlines. — VERIFIED: traced formatter.rs:445-450 (decorator, `newline()`, `write_indent()`, then `write_signature("to ", ...)`) through parser.rs:1230/1244 (next token must be `To`) and lexer.rs:488 (Newline is a token); no formatter test formats a cached function (no `lru_cache` in tests/formatter_test.rs).

2. [MAJOR] src/parser.rs:3203 — `open_block_depth`, which the REPL uses via `needs_continuation` (src/repl/mod.rs:1059-1063), counts an opener only when `at_statement_start`; `lru_cache` is an `Identifier` that clears that flag, so the following `to` is not counted. Typing `lru_cache(10) to f(n)` at the prompt yields depth 0, runs the incomplete declaration, and the buffer is cleared on the parse error. — Teach `open_block_depth` that a leading `lru_cache` (optionally parenthesised) makes the following `to` an opener, and add the form to `edge_every_block_form_asks_for_its_continuation_line`.

3. [MAJOR] src/bytecode/vm.rs:2837 — Bytecode inserts a cache entry only in `return_value` when the return is the block's final instruction; a body that ends in an ordinary expression (or simply reaches the end of its block) is finished by `unwind_frame` (src/bytecode/vm.rs:1656-1682), which returns the value without inserting it. The tree-walker caches every successful result (src/interpreter.rs:3083-3087), and SPEC.md says a miss "records its answer". `lru_cache(10) to f(n) set calls to calls + 1; n end` therefore memoises on `rb run` but not on a compiled run, and the observable call count diverges. — Insert the popped value in `unwind_frame` when `frame.cache` is `Some`, under the same rules as `return_value`.

4. [MAJOR] src/interpreter.rs:2998 — Cache lookup is by `function.name` for every function, while `Statement::Function` only inserts a cache when the declaration is decorated (src/interpreter.rs:2246-2249) and never removes it when a later undecorated declaration rebinds the same name. Thus: decorate `f`, call `f(1)` to populate the cache, redeclare `to f(n) give back n + 100 end`, call `f(1)` again, and both engines return the stale cached answer instead of `101` (bytecode: src/bytecode/vm.rs:2932-2940 and 3211-3213). The same lifetime bug exists across runs because `BytecodeVm::run` clears every other piece of state but not `self.caches` (src/bytecode/vm.rs:1076-1085). — Make the cache a property of the decorated declaration/`FunctionValue`, drop it when the name is rebound undecorated, and clear `self.caches` in `run`.

5. [MAJOR] tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs:1427 — `edge_lru_cache_requires_a_positive_capacity` exercises only `0` and `1.5`; the parser's non-`Number` error branch (src/parser.rs:1286-1291) and the omitted-capacity/default-128 branch (src/parser.rs:1296-1298) have no test, despite the REPORT's edge table claiming "zero, fractional and non-numeric capacities". — Add `lru_cache("x") to f...` and `lru_cache to f...` cases, plus the SPEC claim that a variable named `lru_cache` still works.

6. [MINOR] src/stdlib.rs:1178 — The comment says "A program cannot write the name", but `__lru_cache_declare` is a legal identifier (leading `_` is accepted at src/lexer.rs:515); `to __lru_cache_declare(a, b) ... end` runs the user body in the tree-walker while the bytecode VM intercepts the name before `get_var` (src/bytecode/vm.rs:2880-2902), producing an engine divergence and letting a compiled program register a cache for an undecorated name. — Use a name containing a character the lexer cannot produce, or a dedicated opcode/declaration flag instead of a callable name.

7. [MINOR] SURVEY.md:9 — The re-anchored citations are wrong: `BUILTIN_NAMES` starts at src/stdlib.rs:1187 (not 1209) and `"input"` is at 1279 (not 1308); `filter`/`reduce` are registered at src/stdlib.rs:378-379, not 900 (SURVEY.md:141); `to_text`/`to_number` are at 414-416, not 921 (SURVEY.md:336), and the `to_text` signature is at 1404, not 1039. `SURVEY.md:143-144` also still lists `zip`, `enumerate`, `take` and `skip` as missing although this phase implements them. — Re-anchor to the actual lines and remove those four names from the missing list (or state explicitly that the gap is the list-member spelling, not the flat builtin).

